  "_key": "Typing into the amount field ONE KEY AT A TIME, which `entry` never reached: there the whole string arrives at once, the way Playwright's fill() delivers it, and every row stayed green while Whole dollars turned 1, 4, 5, 0 typed by hand into $1,004,005,000.00 (Sean, 2026-09-30: \"whole dollars is broken on macos\"). The field DRAWS the amount (`amountField`) and the next key arrives as that drawing with the key on it (`amountKeyed` reads it back), so the drawing must read back as the amount it was drawn from, in the same mode. Cents draws the till's '$0.45': its dot is decoration and its digit stream is the cents. Whole dollars never draws a dot nobody typed: bare digits are '$1,450', and a typed dot and its fraction are drawn exactly as typed, '$12.3' not '$12.30', because a key on the end of '$12.30' would be a third decimal. Anything not yet an amount is drawn as the raw digits. `draw` rows are [digits, drawn in cents mode, drawn in Whole dollars]. `read` rows are [mode, the text the field handed back, digits kept] — a paste, or select-all and a key, arrives this way too, as one new string. `keys` rows are [mode, digits already there, keys pressed at the END of the field, what it draws after, cents after]; '⌫' is Backspace. `seed` rows are [a stored amount as `amountInput` writes it, the digits an edit opens with in cents mode, and in Whole dollars]: a round amount opens as '1450' in Whole dollars, so the field draws '$1,450' and the keys after it are dollars. The cents rows pin what the screen already did; they are not a redesign of it.",
